# Introduction

Predicting labor force participation (LFPR) is an essential task for understanding the dynamics of an economy and planning for future labor market needs. Labor force participation—the proportion of the working-age population that is either employed or actively seeking work—serves as a key indicator of economic health, social progress, and demographic change. Accurate forecasts help governments design effective employment policies, social security systems, and education or training programs. For businesses and investors, labor participation predictions inform decisions about production capacity, wage expectations, and workforce availability.

Moreover, shifts in labor force participation can signal broader structural trends, such as aging populations, gender disparities, technological change, or the impact of recessions and policy reforms. By anticipating these shifts, policymakers and economists can better respond to challenges like labor shortages, unemployment cycles, or the transition to automation-driven industries. In short, predicting labor force participation provides critical insight into how human resources evolve alongside economic and social developments.

In this report, we use an ARIMA model to analyze the 
trend of LFPR and predict future events.  We will split the data into a 80/20 
train/test split and predict on the test data.  

